### What is Collectors.partitioningBy?

`Collectors.partitioningBy` takes a `Predicate` and routes each element to the `true` bucket if the predicate holds and the `false` bucket otherwise. The result is always a `Map<Boolean, List<Employee>>` with both keys present.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class SalarySplitter {
    private static final BigDecimal FIFTY_K = new BigDecimal("50000");

    public static Map<Boolean, List<Employee>> splitBySalary(List<Employee> employees) {
        return employees.stream()
                .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(
                        e -> e.getSalary().compareTo(FIFTY_K) > 0));
    }
}
```

### Why does partitioningBy exist when groupingBy can do the same?

You could group by a boolean classifier, but partitioning is the dedicated tool for a binary split. It guarantees both keys are present, so reading code never has to defend against a missing side, and internally it uses a fixed two-slot structure instead of a general hash map.

```java
// groupingBy with a boolean classifier: only creates keys that occur.
Map<Boolean, List<Employee>> grouped = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                e -> e.getSalary().compareTo(FIFTY_K) > 0));
// If no employee is above 50,000, there is NO true key at all.

// partitioningBy: both true and false always exist.
Map<Boolean, List<Employee>> partitioned = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(
                e -> e.getSalary().compareTo(FIFTY_K) > 0));
// true maps to an empty list rather than being absent.
```

### How do you read the two buckets?

Reading is just `result.get(true)` for the employees above 50,000 and `result.get(false)` for everyone at or below 50,000. Because both keys are guaranteed, there is no null check.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class Employee {
    private final String name;
    private final BigDecimal salary;

    public Employee(String name, BigDecimal salary) {
        this.name = name;
        this.salary = salary;
    }

    public String getName() { return name; }
    public BigDecimal getSalary() { return salary; }

    @Override public String toString() { return name; }
}
```

### How do you run a full example?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Employee;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class SalarySplitterDemo {
    private static final BigDecimal FIFTY_K = new BigDecimal("50000");

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = List.of(
                new Employee("Rajesh", new BigDecimal("75000")),
                new Employee("Priya", new BigDecimal("50000")),
                new Employee("Amit", new BigDecimal("42000")),
                new Employee("Pankaj", new BigDecimal("120000")));

        Map<Boolean, List<Employee>> split = employees.stream()
                .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(
                        e -> e.getSalary().compareTo(FIFTY_K) > 0));

        System.out.println("Above 50,000: " + split.get(true));
        System.out.println("50,000 or less: " + split.get(false));
    }
}
```

```bash
javac com/bank/model/Employee.java com/bank/service/SalarySplitterDemo.java
java com.bank.service.SalarySplitterDemo
```

```text
Above 50,000: [Rajesh, Pankaj]
50,000 or less: [Priya, Amit]
```

Note that Priya at exactly 50,000 lands in the `false` bucket because the predicate is strictly greater than.

### How is partitioningBy different from groupingBy?

The core difference is the number of keys and their guaranteed presence.

| Aspect | groupingBy | partitioningBy |
|--------|-----------|----------------|
| Classifier | any function returning any key | a boolean predicate |
| Number of keys | any number, data driven | exactly two: true and false |
| Missing key possible | yes, if no element produces it | no, both always present |
| Result type | `Map<K, List<T>>` | `Map<Boolean, List<T>>` |
| Internal structure | general hash map | fixed two-slot structure |

```java
// groupingBy by department: keys depend on the data.
Map<String, List<Employee>> byDept = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(e -> departmentOf(e)));

// partitioningBy by a rule: always two keys.
Map<Boolean, List<Employee>> byRule = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(
                e -> e.getSalary().compareTo(FIFTY_K) > 0));
```

### How do you add a downstream collector?

Like groupingBy, partitioningBy accepts a downstream collector. `partitioningBy(predicate, counting())` gives `Map<Boolean, Long>` of how many fall on each side.

```java
Map<Boolean, Long> countPerSide = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(
                e -> e.getSalary().compareTo(FIFTY_K) > 0,
                Collectors.counting()));
```

```text
{false=2, true=2}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The biggest money mistake is comparing `BigDecimal` with `==` or converting to `double`. Use `compareTo`. A second mistake is assuming the boundary value lands on the wrong side: 50,000 exactly is not greater than 50,000, so it goes to `false`.

```java
// WRONG: == compares object references, not numeric value.
// e -> e.getSalary() == new BigDecimal("50000")   // always false

// WRONG: double loses precision for money.
// e -> e.getSalary().doubleValue() > 50000.0

// CORRECT: compareTo for exact numeric comparison.
Map<Boolean, List<Employee>> correct = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(
                e -> e.getSalary().compareTo(FIFTY_K) > 0));
```

### What are the edge cases?

An empty stream still returns a map with both keys, each mapping to an empty list. A predicate that is true for every element leaves the `false` key mapped to an empty list, and vice versa. The boundary value depends on whether you use `>` or `>=`.

```java
// Edge case: empty input still has both keys.
Map<Boolean, List<Employee>> emptyResult = List.<Employee>of().stream()
        .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(
                e -> e.getSalary().compareTo(FIFTY_K) > 0));
// emptyResult = {false=[], true=[]}
System.out.println(emptyResult.get(true).isEmpty());  // true, no NPE
```

### When should you use partitioningBy, and when not?

Use partitioningBy whenever the split is genuinely binary and driven by a true-or-false business rule, because the guaranteed both-keys property removes null handling. Do not use it when you have more than two categories, where `groupingBy` fits, and do not use it if a boolean key would be awkward to read downstream, where a named enum classifier with `groupingBy` is clearer.

```java
// Use partitioningBy: a true/false rule.
Map<Boolean, List<Employee>> eligible = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(
                e -> e.getSalary().compareTo(FIFTY_K) > 0));

// Do NOT use partitioningBy: three tiers need groupingBy.
// LOW, MID, HIGH -> use an enum classifier with groupingBy.
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers ask why partitioning guarantees both keys, how it differs from grouping on a boolean, how to count or reduce each side, where the boundary value lands, and why `BigDecimal.compareTo` is required for money. A senior answer adds that partitioningBy is slightly faster because it avoids boxing a `Boolean` into a general map and uses a fixed two-slot container.

```java
// Follow-up: total salary on each side using a reducing downstream.
import java.math.BigDecimal;

Map<Boolean, BigDecimal> totalPerSide = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(
                e -> e.getSalary().compareTo(FIFTY_K) > 0,
                Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO,
                        Employee::getSalary, BigDecimal::add)));
```
